# Monthly restore drill over the newest D1 backups (fleet-ops#10594).
#
# What it proves: an encrypted dump that sits in a GitHub artifact really can be
# turned back into a database, on the host that already holds the private key,
# without a Cloudflare token and without creating anything in Cloudflare.
# fleet-ops#10594 asked for exactly this, because a backup nobody has restored
# is a claim, not a backup.
#
# What it does, in four steps and no script:
#   1. Reads the newest completed run of drive's weekly d1-export.yml and lists the
#      d1-backup-* artifacts it left behind. No branch filter: a run dispatched by
#      hand from a branch exports the same live database as the weekly one, so the
#      newest artifact is the newest backup whichever branch made it, and the
#      drill works from the day the workflow lands rather than from the week the
#      PR merges. One date is written once here (IST) and every later step reads
#      it, so a catch-up run that crosses midnight still names the report with
#      one string.
#   2. Downloads each of those artifacts with gh, as the nishfleet-worker App
#      with a one-hour Actions-write token for that one repo
#      (fleet-worker-github-token@actions:drive.service, the same unit
#      fleet-pr-sweep@.service uses). Actions write is what carries actions read;
#      the App has no narrower grant, and the token is revoked and its env file
#      deleted the moment the run ends, so a detached worker on the same uid
#      never finds a live one in the runtime dir between drills.
#   3. Decrypts each dump with age and the private key at
#      ~/.config/drive/d1-backup-key.txt, loads the SQL into a fresh local
#      SQLite file, runs PRAGMA integrity_check, and counts every table's rows.
#   4. Writes one report per month to ~/workspaces/agent-state/d1-restore-drill/.
#
# What it deliberately does not do: it never calls the Cloudflare API. The
# restore target is a local SQLite file, so the drill cannot create, import
# into or delete a D1 database, and it needs no D1 token at all. That is the
# point of putting it here: the private key lives on this host and on no
# runner, and a GitHub Actions job could not reach it without a secret that
# Nish has ruled out (fleet-ops#10594).
#
# The dumps are real customer data (payment rows, email signups). They are
# decrypted only inside a 0700 runtime directory under /run/user (tmpfs), and
# systemd removes that directory when the unit stops, so nothing decrypted
# outlives the drill. The report carries counts and integrity results, never a
# row, a name or an email address.
#
# A database that has no working backup shows up as a missing artifact, not as a
# silent pass: step 1 fails when the newest run left no d1-backup-* artifact at
# all, and the report lists only what was actually restored. Today
# tinystudio_email_signups has no artifact because its export answers HTTP 404
# (fleet-ops#10830) and the drive database has none because the D1 export API
# refuses FTS5 virtual tables (drive#1027); both stay visible in the run's own
# job list instead of being hidden here.
#
# Gates, each a failed unit someone can list (OnFailure= pages): a completed
# run exists, at least one artifact exists, each download succeeds, each
# integrity_check answers ok, at least one database was restored, and the
# report exists. A dump that decrypts but does not load fails the unit, because
# that is the failure this drill exists to catch.
#
# %%, $$, and no printf escapes. systemd expands % and $ itself before any shell
# runs, so every shell $ is written $$ and every shell % is written %%
# (ci.yml gate fleet-ops#8382), and systemd also unescapes a backslash pair
# before the shell sees it. echo carries the newlines instead of printf, so no
# backslash reaches the shell at all except the two a SQL identifier needs,
# written \\" the way fleet-grafana-github-token.service writes them. gh is
# found through /usr/bin/env, so the unit names no home path.
#
# Switch off: systemctl --user disable --now fleet-d1-restore-drill.timer, then
# drop step 2l and its enable line from fleet-sync.service and delete both files.
[Unit]
Description=Monthly restore drill over the newest D1 backups (fleet-ops#10594)
OnFailure=fleet-unit-failed@%N.service
Requires=fleet-worker-github-token@actions:drive.service
After=fleet-worker-github-token@actions:drive.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RuntimeDirectory=fleet-d1-restore-drill
RuntimeDirectoryMode=0700
Environment=GH_REPO=Nishfleet/drive
Environment=GH_WORKFLOW=d1-export.yml
Environment=DRILL_KEY=%h/.config/drive/d1-backup-key.txt
Environment=DRILL_STATE=%h/workspaces/agent-state/d1-restore-drill
Environment=TZ=Asia/Kolkata
Environment=LC_ALL=C.UTF-8
EnvironmentFile=%t/fleet-worker-github-token/actions:drive.env
# The required EnvironmentFile above is loaded for every command below, so an
# empty token means a failed mint and the run must not fall back to the owner's
# gh login. $$ keeps systemd from expanding the token into the shell's argv.
ExecStartPre=/bin/sh -c 'test -n "$$GH_TOKEN" || { echo "drill: no GitHub App token, refusing to run" >&2; exit 1; }'
# 1. The newest completed backup run, its artifacts, and the one date. A run that
#    ended in failure still holds the artifacts of the jobs that succeeded, which
#    is why the newest completed run is the right one and not the newest success.
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'set -eu; umask 077; out="$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY"; \
  d=$$(date +%%F); echo "$$d" > "$$out/date"; \
  test -s "$$DRILL_KEY" || { echo "drill: the private key $$DRILL_KEY is missing" >&2; exit 1; }; \
  run=$$(gh run list -R $$GH_REPO -w $$GH_WORKFLOW -s completed -L 1 --json databaseId -q ".[0].databaseId"); \
  test -n "$$run" || { echo "drill: no completed $$GH_WORKFLOW run in $$GH_REPO" >&2; exit 1; }; \
  echo "$$run" > "$$out/run"; \
  gh api "repos/$$GH_REPO/actions/runs/$$run/artifacts?per_page=100" --paginate -q ".artifacts[].name" | grep "^d1-backup-" > "$$out/names" || true; \
  test -s "$$out/names" || { echo "drill: run $$run left no d1-backup-* artifact" >&2; exit 1; }; \
  echo "drill: backup run $$run on $$d, $$(wc -l < "$$out/names") dump(s): $$(tr "\n" " " < "$$out/names")"'
# 2. One download per artifact, straight out of the run.
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'set -eu; umask 077; out="$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY"; run=$$(cat "$$out/run"); mkdir -p "$$out/dumps"; \
  while read -r a; do test -n "$$a" || continue; gh run download "$$run" -R $$GH_REPO -n "$$a" -D "$$out/dumps/$$a"; echo "drill: downloaded $$a"; done < "$$out/names"'
# 3. Decrypt, load into a fresh local SQLite file, integrity-check, count rows.
#    The D1 export API hands back SQL text, not a database file, so the restore
#    is a load into an empty database: that is the step a backup claim skips.
#    The SQL is double-quoted (type = \\"table\\") because a systemd Exec line is
#    itself single-quoted, so no shell single quote can appear in it, and SQLite
#    reads a double-quoted string that names no identifier as a string.
#    sqlite_sequence is SQLite's own AUTOINCREMENT bookkeeping, not application
#    data, so it is not counted among the tables the dump made.
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'set -eu; umask 077; out="$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY"; : > "$$out/rows"; : > "$$out/tables"; n=0; \
  for f in "$$out"/dumps/*/*.sql.age; do \
    test -e "$$f" || { echo "drill: no dump found under $$out/dumps" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    a=$$(basename "$$(dirname "$$f")"); b=$$(basename "$$f" .sql.age); db="$$out/$$b.db"; \
    age -d -i "$$DRILL_KEY" -o "$$out/$$b.sql" "$$f"; \
    rm -f "$$db"; sqlite3 "$$db" < "$$out/$$b.sql"; \
    ic=$$(sqlite3 "$$db" "PRAGMA integrity_check;"); \
    test "$$ic" = ok || { echo "drill: $$a did not restore: integrity_check answered $$ic" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    tot=0; det=""; \
    for t in $$(sqlite3 "$$db" "select name from sqlite_master where type = \\"table\\" and name not like \\"sqlite_%%\\" order by name"); do \
      c=$$(sqlite3 "$$db" "select count(*) from \\"$$t\\""); tot=$$((tot+c)); det="$$det $$t=$$c"; done; \
    echo "| $$b | $$a | $$(wc -c < "$$out/$$b.sql") | ok | $$tot |" >> "$$out/rows"; \
    echo "- $$b:$$det" >> "$$out/tables"; \
    n=$$((n+1)); echo "drill: restored $$a from $$b, $$tot rows, integrity ok"; \
  done; \
  test "$$n" -gt 0 || { echo "drill: nothing was restored" >&2; exit 1; }; \
  echo "$$n" > "$$out/count"'
# 4. One report, in agent-state, which is private and never reaches a public repo.
#    Counts and integrity results only: no row, no name, no email address.
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'set -eu; umask 077; out="$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY"; d=$$(cat "$$out/date"); run=$$(cat "$$out/run"); \
  mkdir -p "$$DRILL_STATE"; rep="$$DRILL_STATE/report-$$d.md"; \
  { echo "# D1 restore drill, $$d (Asia/Kolkata)"; echo; \
    echo "Backup run: https://github.com/$$GH_REPO/actions/runs/$$run"; \
    echo "Source: the weekly $$GH_WORKFLOW matrix in $$GH_REPO (fleet-ops#10594)."; \
    echo "Key: $$DRILL_KEY"; \
    echo "Where: this host, into a 0700 runtime directory that systemd removes when the unit ends. No Cloudflare database was created and no Cloudflare API call was made."; echo; \
    echo "| database | artifact | dump bytes | integrity | rows |"; echo "|---|---|---|---|---|"; \
    cat "$$out/rows"; echo; echo "Per table:"; cat "$$out/tables"; echo; \
    echo "$$(cat "$$out/count") database(s) restored."; \
  } > "$$rep"; \
  echo "drill: report $$rep"'
# The report is the deliverable. It is checked against the date file rather than
# a fresh `date`, so a run that crosses midnight still reads its own report, and
# the message names which step produced nothing instead of only no-deliverable.
ExecStopPost=/bin/sh -c 'out="$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY"; test -s "$$out/date" || { echo "no-deliverable: set-up never wrote its date, so the run stopped before the run list" >&2; exit 1; }; test -s "$$out/run" || { echo "no-deliverable: set-up stopped after the date write: no completed backup run, or the artifact list was empty" >&2; exit 1; }; test -d "$$out/dumps" || { echo "no-deliverable: no artifact was downloaded" >&2; exit 1; }; test -s "$$out/count" || { echo "no-deliverable: no dump was decrypted, loaded and integrity-checked" >&2; exit 1; }; rep="$$DRILL_STATE/report-$$(cat "$$out/date").md"; test -s "$$rep" || { echo "no-deliverable: the restore ran but wrote no report" >&2; exit 1; }; echo "drill: report $$rep"'
# The revoke and the rm are one command, because systemd loads EnvironmentFile=
# again for every command it spawns: a revoke after the rm could not start, and a
# failed command skips the rest. The revoke runs only with the App token loaded
# (an empty GH_TOKEN would fall back to the owner's login), the rm always runs,
# and a failed revoke still fails the unit.
ExecStopPost=/bin/sh -c 'rc=0; [ -z "$$GH_TOKEN" ] || /usr/bin/env gh api -X DELETE installation/token || rc=$$?; rm -f %t/fleet-worker-github-token/actions:drive.env; exit $$rc'
TimeoutStartSec=1800
